+# Results so far (2026-10-21)
+
+Plan: [RESEARCH_PLAN.md](RESEARCH_PLAN.md). This page lists what the 2026-10-15/16 run established and how
+strongly.
+- **Verified** means two independent implementations agree **and** adversarial checkers could not break the
+  claim. Every checker so far was written by a Claude model; the OpenCode route to a non-Claude implementation
+  failed.
+- **Computed once** means one program produced the number.
+- **Proved** means one model wrote a proof. No second model or human has re-derived it yet.
+
+**Conventions (decided 2026-10-16).**
+- A pawn that can still be taken en passant is **not** passed: `cylcgt.pawns.PASSED_RESPECTS_EP = True`.
+  - Merged into `main` on 2026-10-21 (commit d3d080e). `PASSED_RESPECTS_EP = False` restores the old test.
+  - Results below that predate this switch are marked *(textbook test)*.
+- Kings are modelled explicitly in `kings/` (plan item A8; see §7).
+
+## 1. The \*2 question (Elkies' open problem): the candidate does not survive
+
+The candidate was `W a2 c2 / B b7 d7` on files a–d, frozen by a trébuchet (W g4 / B g5, WK f5, BK h4).
+
+| Check | Result | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| Value under the paper's convention, textbook passed test | \*2 under race rules "count" and "mover"; **0** under "opponent". The same with e.p. off | verified (cylcgt, `sumtest/`, Codex rules in `atlas/flat8/census`, `lemmas/`) |
+| Value under the adopted convention (e.p.-aware passed test) | **{2±1 \| −2±1}** (mean 0, temperature 2), not \*2. Mechanism: a2–a4 is met by b4×a3 e.p. | verified: cylcgt now agrees with `sumtest/`'s e.p.-aware mode on all 55,195 test positions (§4) |
+| Robustness of the \*2 to the race rule | only 4 of its 224 race positions change the value when flipped alone; all come after a capture. A 6-position certificate fixes \*2 whatever the other races do | verified (`atlas/flat8/certificate.json`, `lemmas/` L4.2) |
+| Sum model vs whole-board game | equal: the tree never splits into components before a passer (Theorem 2.3) | proved |
+| 8×8 embedding geometry (kings f5/h4) | no checks, no stalemate, every king move drops that side's g-pawn | proved; exhaustive check |
+| Real chess, both sides to move (Lichess 7-man tablebase + exact search) | the side to move wins (outcome N), as \*2 predicts | proved by certificate (`realchess/results/cert_root_*.json`) |
+| Real-chess oracle value: the pawn tree with tablebase results at its end positions | **never \*2 (or 0) in the adopted e.p.-aware tree, however draws and unresolved end positions are treated**: the bounds force Left stop ≥ 1 and Right stop ≤ −1. Counting draws as Black wins, it is **exactly {2±1 \| −2±1}** in both trees. In the textbook tree \*2 is excluded only when draws count as wins for one side | values computed once; end-position results checked two ways ([realchess/REPORT.md](realchess/REPORT.md)) |
+| Real-chess root move tables | Black to move: only 1...b5 wins (complete). White to move: 1.c4 wins; 1.a3, 1.c3 and all king moves lose; 1.a4 unresolved. The model predicts exactly c4 and b5 | checked two ways |
+| The same FEN on the 8-file cylinder | the freeze fails: 1.a3 checks Kh4 across the seam. No \*k (k ≥ 2) with ≤ 5 pawns can be frozen on the 8-file cylinder (0 of 40 placements) | computed once (`atlas/cylinder/`) |
+
+**Why it fails.** The convention "first passed pawn wins" breaks in three places:
+- **En passant.** A pawn that double-steps past an enemy pawn counted as passed before the opponent's legal
+  e.p. capture. This is now fixed in cylcgt, and the \*2 goes away with it.
+- **Frozen kings.** In real chess the trébuchet kings are not always frozen. Across 94 tablebase-checked
+  positions, 41 of 449 king moves draw and 7 win (`engine/suite/`).
+- **Race rules.** None of count, mover or opponent is exact chess. `notes/lemmas.md` L4 gives a forced-win
+  counterexample for each.
+
+**Searching for other \*k (flat 8-rank boards, widths 1–4, up to 6 pawns, 231,071 positions).**
+- *Textbook test:* the only \*k with k ≥ 2 is the candidate above. No \*3 or \*4 occurs. No \*2 holds under all
+  six race/e.p. settings. Three more \*2s exist, each under a single non-default setting
+  ([atlas/flat8/REPORT.md](atlas/flat8/REPORT.md); verified two ways).
+- *E.p.-aware test (count rule; computed once, `atlas/flat8/compare_ep_aware.out`):*
+  - **No \*k with k ≥ 2 occurs anywhere in scope.** The nimber census goes from 6,470 zeros, 3,891 stars and one
+    \*2 to 6,724 zeros, 4,042 stars and no \*2.
+  - At least 18,137 of the 231,071 values change, and 4,070 change outcome ([atlas/flat8/rerun/REPORT.md](atlas/flat8/rerun/REPORT.md)).
+    The first count (21,441 and 4,068) compared raw display strings and was wrong in both directions.
+  - So within flat boards of ≤4 files and ≤6 pawns, the only route to \*2 was the e.p. artefact.
+
+**The other \*2 candidates** ([realchess/certify/REPORT.md](realchess/certify/REPORT.md)). Each is \*2 only
+under one non-default rule, and each needs 10 men.
+- `W a3 b3 c2 / B b5 b7 c6` is **refuted**: its real-chess oracle value is > 0 under every treatment (checked two
+  ways).
+- For three others the tablebase contradicts the race verdicts their \*2 depends on. Their oracle values cannot
+  be settled with 7-man tablebases.
+- No \*k candidate fits in 7 men or fewer.
+- An independent C solver (`atlas/flat8/rerun/`, no shared code) agrees with cylcgt on all 231,071 records under
+  both passed tests (checked two ways).
+
+**What this means.** A \*2 made only of kings and pawns on 8×8 is **still open**. Within the searched space
+(flat, ≤4 files, ≤6 pawns) no candidate survives the e.p.-aware convention, and none survives real chess. The
+negative result is publishable: it shows exactly where the convention breaks.
+
+## 2. Mating without corners: 4-man tablebases on the cylinder and torus (verified)
+
+Two independent solvers agree on every position of all 24 tables, about 607 million values, for both sides
+to move ([tablebase/replica/REPORT.md](tablebase/replica/REPORT.md)).
+
+| Material (cylinder 8×8) | White to move: share won | Longest mate (moves) |
+|---|---|---|
+| KQK | 100% | 10 |
+| KRK | 2.80%: **no forced mate** | 3 |
+| KBBK, opposite-coloured bishops | **100%** | **14** |
+| KBNK | 0.0245%: **draw** | 2 |
+| KNNK | 0.0077% | 1 |
+| KRBK | 99.74% | 13 |
+| KRRK | 100% | 8 |
+| KQRK | 100% | 5 |
+
+- **Torus.** KQ, KR, KBB, KBN and KNN against a lone king are all 0% won, and KRB vs K is 0.022%.
+- **Muller.** The bishop pair forcing mate on the cylinder contradicts H.G. Muller's published claim.
+- **Novelty.** We found no earlier tablebase for these boards.
+- **King and pawn vs king** (two solvers: `kings/kpsolve` and `atlas/cylinder/kpk`; they agree on all 326,640
+  cylinder positions, and on the flat board differ only on the 6 rook-underpromotion positions;
+  `delegate/checks/kpk_crosscheck.py`).
+  - **Win rates.** White to move wins 78.89% on the cylinder against 76.51% flat. Black to move: 55.88%
+    against 58.09%.
+  - **Changed results.** 16,538 flat draws become cylinder wins, among them the rook-pawn draw Ka6, Pa5 vs Ka8,
+    and 15,930 flat wins become cylinder draws.
+  - **Mutual zugzwangs.** 216 on the cylinder, 160 flat.
+
+## 3. The 2025 paper, re-audited (verified)
+
+A blind re-transcription of every diagram matches the solver's input on every pawn square. A pixel scan
+confirmed it independently. The kings, and diagrams 2.3 and 5.1, were checked against the images only
+([notes/diagram_audit.md](notes/diagram_audit.md)). Three verifiers checked each audit claim. Corrected
+verdicts are in [notes/audit.md](notes/audit.md):
+- **Correct:**
+  - 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.2, 4.1, 4.3;
+  - 5.1, the cylinder trébuchet: a mutual zugzwang **only because of the seam**, and a real one with explicit kings (two independent king-and-pawn solvers);
+  - the 7.1 c- and f-files;
+  - the 7.2 king zugzwang, but **only under the convention**. In real chess, White to move draws with Kd5, Ke5 or
+    Kf5, and only Black to move loses (`kings/kpsolve`, confirmed by the Lichess tablebase).
+- **Correct only with en passant:** 3.1.
+- **Wrong:**
+  - 4.2 is one rank off.
+  - 7.1 a/h is {+W \| \*}, not 1.
+  - 7.2's pawn part is not ±1.
+  - 7.3 is decided at move 0.
+  - 7.3's {c,d} part is tiny(1), not 1.
+  - §1's knight squares are wrong.
+- **§8, Dawson's chess, is wrong in three ways** (verified, `dawson/`):
+  - its move rule allows no first move;
+  - its Grundy recurrence matches the true values at only 31 of n = 0..300;
+  - its cylinder outcomes are wrong for 56 values of n ≤ 300.
+
+  Replacement text: `dawson/section8_corrected.tex`.
+
+Every value in the audit is **unchanged** under the e.p.-aware convention (re-run 2026-10-16).
+
+## 4. The solver itself
+
+- **Agreement with an independent solver** (verified). `cylcgt` agrees with a from-scratch brute-force solver
+  (`sumtest/`) on every outcome prediction. The test set:
+  - 55,195 positions (≤2 pawns a side, 3–5 files), × 3 race rules × 15 reference games;
+  - e.p. on: 2,483,775 predictions under the adopted e.p.-aware test (both sum modes) and the same number
+    under the textbook test;
+  - e.p. off: 2,483,775 predictions.
+- **Single components.** It agrees with Codex's whole-board minimax on 1,070/1,070 of them (`crosscheck/`).
+- **Three defects found, all fixed or documented:**
+  - **En passant in the passed-pawn test.** Fixed: `PASSED_RESPECTS_EP`.
+  - **`sum_safe()` is a heuristic, not a certificate** (checked two ways, `notes/lemmas.md` L2.2).
+    - Example: `W b2 d4 g3 h5 / B a7 c7 f7 g4` on the 8×8 cylinder (e.p. off) passes, yet the sum model gets
+      the winner wrong.
+    - The proved replacement is the "hereditarily sum-safe" test (`lemmas/common.py`, Theorem 2.1). All 1,297
+      such positions in a random sample of 1,500 agree.
+  - **An e.p. right can survive moves elsewhere** in a multi-component sum. The sum is exact only when no
+    position with two or more components carries a usable e.p. right (condition (E), L1.6–1.7).
+- **Not a solver defect, a misuse.** The partizan values that `cylcgt/dawson.py` prints are not additive. The
+  Grundy value of octal 0.137 is the correct invariant (L5.4).
+
+## 5. Engines
+
+- [engine/ENGINES.md](engine/ENGINES.md) makes 16 predictions about how Stockfish and Lc0 search fails on
+  zugzwang, tempo battles and nimbers, and designs 8 experiments.
+  - Stockfish's null-move pruning never fires in king-and-pawn endings, because it requires pieces.
+  - Reverse futility pruning is the predicted zugzwang-blind cut.
+- **Test suite.** Both files are tablebase-checked legal 8×8 positions:
+  - `engine/suite/cgt_verified.epd`: **181 positions** whose winning moves follow from CGT and are confirmed
+    by the Lichess tablebase;
+  - `engine/suite/cgt_disagree.epd`: the 52 positions where CGT and the tablebase disagree.
+- **Stockfish 19 on the suite.** Official release `sf_19`, built locally in `engine/vendor/` (git-ignored);
+  1 thread, 64 MB hash, no tablebases. Runs and analysis are in `engine/runs/`.
+  - **It solves all 233 positions at 100k nodes, and 232/233 already at 1k nodes.** At ≤7 men these
+    positions are easy for a modern engine. The CGT-versus-engine contrast has to come from larger sums,
+    beyond the tablebase, where only CGT gives ground truth. That is the ChessSums benchmark.
+  - **At shallow depth, accuracy depends on the CGT value class.** The table gives the share of Stockfish
+    evaluations that match the tablebase result:
+
+| CGT class | n | depth 1 | depth 2 | depth 4 | depth 6 | depth 8 | depth 12 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| hot (±BIG switch) | 68 | 81% | 78% | 91% | 97% | 99% | 100% |
+| number | 57 | 79% | 81% | 84% | 93% | 95% | 98% |
+| switch | 37 | 73% | 78% | 89% | 100% | 100% | 100% |
+| **infinitesimal** | 27 | **30%** | **26%** | **59%** | 85% | 100% | 100% |
+| other tame | 20 | 60% | 50% | 75% | 95% | 100% | 100% |
+| **nimber** | 17 | **53%** | 65% | 88% | 100% | 100% | 100% |
+
+  Infinitesimals and nimbers, which are tempo and parity fights, are the ones the static evaluation
+  misreads at low depth. CGT predicts exactly that. Hot positions and numbers are read correctly early.
+  This is one engine on a small sample, so it is a pilot result.
+- **Cylinder engine.** The only public cylinder fork of Fairy-Stockfish (mtaktikos/CylindricalChess)
+  appears, from reading its code, to miss checks, pins and en passant across the seam.
+  [engine/FAIRY_CYLINDER.md](engine/FAIRY_CYLINDER.md) gives a corrected design.
+- **Lc0 v0.32.1 on the suite** (bundled network, Metal; [engine/runs/lc0/analysis.md](engine/runs/lc0/analysis.md)).
+  - **Perfect at every budget.** At 1k, 10k, 100k and 1M nodes all 233 best moves win and all 233 evals match the
+    tablebase, in every CGT class.
+  - **Lc0 never shows the shallow-depth class effect** that Stockfish shows. Caveat: 216 of the 233 positions are
+    wins, so the 17 draws are the real test. Lc0 calls all 17 draws at 1k nodes; Stockfish 19 at 1k rates one +183.
+  - **The suite is too easy for both engines.** That again points to ChessSums beyond 7 men for a real contrast.
+
+## 6. Other new results (model level unless stated)
+
+- **Elkies' other targets on flat 8-rank boards** ([atlas/flat8/REPORT.md](atlas/flat8/REPORT.md)). Counts are
+  under the textbook test (verified two ways) → the e.p.-aware test (count rule, computed once):
+
+  | Target | Textbook | E.p.-aware | Example that survives the switch |
+  |---|---|---|---|
+  | 1/8 | 13 | 9 | `W a2 b4 c4 / B a7 b7 d6` |
+  | 1/16 | 3 | 2 | `W a2 b2 c3 / B b5 b6 c7` |
+  | 1/32 | 1 | **0** | — |
+  | ↑² = {0 \| ↓\*} | 5 | 2 | `W a2 b2 d4 / B a7 c6 d7` |
+  | ↑²\* = {0,\* \| ↓} | 10 | 10 | `W a2 b2 b6 / B a6 b7 c5`: the same under every race rule and e.p. setting, under both tests |
+  | tiny(1) | 13 | 16 | `W a2 b2 b6 / B a6 b7 c6` |
+
+  - **Two textbook-test results did not survive the switch:**
+    - The "best reachable 1/8", `W a2 b2 d2 / B a5 c4 d7`, becomes −3/2±3/2.
+    - The best ↑², `W a3 c5 d4 / B a7 b7 d7`, becomes {3/4 \| ↓\*}.
+  - **The most robust target is 1/8.** `W a2 a3 b2 / B a6 b5 b6` is 1/8 under all 9 settings (3 race rules × e.p.
+    off / textbook / aware) and every race outcome (checked two ways, `atlas/flat8/rerun/`). Its mirror
+    `W a2 b2 b3 / B a5 a6 b6` has the same value and a reachable pawn structure. 8×8 FEN with the trébuchet:
+    `8/8/pp6/p4Kp1/6Pk/1P6/PP6/8 w` (10 men). This is model-level only, not real-chess certified.
+  - ↑²\* at `W a2 b2 b6 / B a6 b7 c5` is equally robust.
+  - The other surviving positions pass necessary 8×8 legality conditions only.
+  - tiny(x) for non-integer x and tiny(1↑) do not occur under either test. tiny(2) appears under the e.p.-aware test.
+- **Cylinder value atlas** ([atlas/cylinder/REPORT.md](atlas/cylinder/REPORT.md); computed once, outcomes
+  cross-checked by an independent minimax):
+  - **Scope:** 1,223,689 positions (≤5 pawns, widths 3–8) with 10,369 distinct values.
+  - **Cut lemma (proved):** cutting a cylinder at an empty file gives a flat board with the same value. So
+    cylinder-only values come only from rings, positions with a pawn on every file.
+  - **Cylinder-only values:** 53 values occur only on cylinders, among them 3/4 and 1/4±1/4. All come from 3- and
+    4-file rings.
+  - **Corrected by a verifier:** the atlas claimed that none of these values is robust to the race rule.
+    - The 3-file ring values do depend on the race rule.
+    - The 4-file ring values, such as {3/2±1/2 \| −3/2±1/2}, do not; they change only when e.p. is switched off.
+    - No value is cylinder-only under all six race/e.p. settings.
+  - All of this uses the textbook passed test. The re-run under the e.p.-aware test is pending.
+- **Dawson's chess** ([dawson/REPORT.md](dawson/REPORT.md)):
+# Research Plan: Cylindrical Chess Endgames, Exactly and with Learning
+
+*Continuation of "Analysis of Chess Endgames on a Cylindrical Chessboard" (A. Bhattacharjee, mentored by T. Morley, June 2025). Drafted 2026-10-15 by Claude (Opus) with Codex (gpt-6-luna) and three research subagents.*
+
+---
+
+## 0. Summary
+
+The 2025 paper applied Elkies-style combinatorial game theory (CGT) to pawn endgames on an 8×8 board whose a- and h-files are glued. Its hand computations stop where game trees get large, and several are wrong (§1).
+
+The literature search (§2) found **no published CGT work on cylindrical or toroidal chessboards**, and **no published tablebases for them**. Elkies' own open problems about the 8×8 board are mostly still open.
+
+The continuation is a **machine-verified research program** with three tracks:
+
+1. **Exact mathematics (Track A).** A verified CGT engine for pawn endgames on cylinders, flat boards and tori of any width. It will (a) prove the structural lemmas the paper assumes, (b) map which values occur only on a cylinder, (c) attack Elkies' open problems (\*2 from kings and pawns alone on 8×8, \*4, 1/8, ↑²), and (d) extend the analysis to king moves (loopy games) and temperature.
+2. **The first cylinder and torus tablebases (Track B).** They settle "with no corners, what mates still work?" and supply ground truth. A 4-man prototype already answers part of this (§1.3).
+3. **Learning against exact truth (Track C).**
+   - Sums of many small games are trivial for CGT but exponential for search. That makes them a principled adversarial benchmark for Stockfish, Leela and LLMs. The closest prior work (Husna & Müller 2024, Go) never varied the number of components.
+   - The same positions test whether neural nets and RL-trained LLMs learn the algebra of sums.
+
+Each track can yield its own paper. Targets: *Integers* / *Games of No Chance 7* (A), *ICGA Journal* / *Advances in Computer Games* (B), and a NeurIPS/ICLR workshop (C).
+
+---
+
+## 1. What exists after today
+
+### 1.1 Tools built this session
+| Path | What it is | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| `cylcgt/cgt.py` | Exact canonical-form CGT: numbers, nimbers, ↑-family, tinies, switches, sums, outcome classes, thermographs (mean, temperature) | Unit-tested (`tests/`) |
+| `cylcgt/pawns.py` | Pawn-only positions on any W-file board, flat or cylindrical; double steps; wrap-around en passant; decomposition into independent components; explicit race rule; e.p.-aware passed test; `sum_safe()` heuristic (not a certificate; see `notes/lemmas.md` L2.2) | Cross-checked (below) |
+| `cylcgt/verify_paper.py` | Recomputes every pawn-only claim of the 2025 paper | Output in `notes/verify_paper_output.txt` |
+| `cylcgt/explore.py` | Exhaustive enumeration of value spectra, cylinder vs flat | Prototype (~0.6 ms per position) |
+| `cylcgt/dawson.py` | Dawson's chess solved directly from the pawn rules, flat and ring | Prototype |
+| `crosscheck/` | **Independent** whole-board minimax written by Codex (it does not use `cylcgt`) | **All 1070 single-component positions agree; 99.5% of sums agree** |
+| `tablebase/egt_prototype.c` | 4-man retrograde DTM solver for plane, cylinder and torus | Reproduces known flat-board maxima |
+
+**Cross-check, in detail.** Run on 3,028 random and paper positions:
+- After one bug fix (a passed pawn must end the whole game before splitting into components), single components agree **100% (1070/1070)**.
+- The 10 remaining disagreements, all in multi-component sums, are the known "+BIG and −BIG cancel" limitation. Details are in `crosscheck/REPORT_after_fix.md`.
+
+### 1.2 Audit of the 2025 paper (full table: `notes/audit.md`)
+- **Correct:** 2.1, 2.2, 3.2, 4.1, 4.3 (tiny-1), the 7.1 c-file (±1), and the 7.1 f-file ({{2|1}|0}, mean = temperature = 3/4).
+- **Correct only because of wrap-around en passant**, which the paper never mentions: 3.1 is `1` with e.p. and `2` without.
+- **Wrong:**
+  - **4.2 as drawn is `0`, not ↑.** The diagram is one rank off: W e2 / B e5 gives ↑.
+  - **The 7.1 a/h component is a hot game, `{win | *}`, not `1`.** The paper plays h6×a5 correctly; its error is calling a position White wins either way "1". Two independent engines refute "= 1" by sum tests.
+  - **7.3's {c,d} sub-game is tiny(1), not 1** (blind re-audit, `notes/diagram_audit.md`).
+  - **7.2's pawn group is not ±1**, and its value depends on en passant. Its analysis also omits the double steps and plays ...b5 twice.
+  - **7.3 is already decided at move 0** by the passed pawns on a5 and f7.
+- **Modelling gaps:**
+  - The 7.3 king group is not a mutual zugzwang: Kg8–f8 and Kf6–g6 are spare moves. The 7.2 group (Ke6, d7 / Kd8, e7) **is** one under the convention, as is 5.1 across the seam (corrected by the blind diagram re-audit).
+  - Pawns stand on the 1st rank, which is illegal.
+  - Races and en passant inside sums are left undefined.
+  - §8's Dawson rule permits no first move. The correct model is octal 0.137, and the paper's path recurrence omits the end moves.
+  - The Wu–Yü–Landry citation is dated 2020 but is from 2010.
+- **Model errors we caught:** Codex's referee report wrongly says that 4.3 has no black reply (a6×h5 exists), that Kh5–g5 is "safe" in the trebuchet (it drops a4), and that 3.1 has a pawn on h4. This is why every claim is machine-checked.
+
+### 1.3 First new results (preliminary; each needs the verification protocol in §4)
+1. **Candidate \*2 made only of kings and pawns on an 8×8 board.**
+   - On files a–d with the e-file empty, `W a2 c2 / B b7 d7` has value **\*2** = {0,\*|0,\*}, with or without en passant.
+   - Elkies (1996) left open whether \*2 occurs on 8×8. Drummond-Cole (2005) found one, but it needs bishops, knights, rooks and queens to freeze the pawns.
+   - If this survives with real kings (the trebuchet on the g-file, kings on f/h) under engine and tablebase verification, **it answers an open question.**
+   - Risk: the "passed pawn = win" convention may not survive real king play.
+   - **Outcome (2026-10-16): refuted.** The \*2 needs the textbook passed test. Under the adopted e.p.-aware test the value is {2±1 | −2±1}. The real-chess oracle also excludes \*2. See RESULTS.md §1.
+2. **Values that occur only on the cylinder.** Exhaustive 2-vs-2 enumeration on 3–5 files finds values that never occur on flat strips of the same width, e.g. 1/4±1/4, 3/4, and {3/2±1/2 | −3/2±1/2}. Some flat-only values (including that \*2) disappear on the cylinder.
+3. **Mating material without corners** (4-man tablebase). **Replicated:** an independent solver, `tablebase/replica/replica.c`, written and committed before the prototype was read, agrees with the prototype on every position of all 24 tables (about 607 million values) for both sides to move; see `tablebase/replica/REPORT.md`. New tables there: KRRK and KQRK are forced wins on the cylinder (100%, longest mate in 8 and 5 moves).
+
+| Material | Flat 8×8 | Cylinder 8×8 | Torus 8×8 |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| KQK | win, mate in ≤ 10 | win, mate in ≤ 10 | **no mate exists** |
+| KRK | win, 16 | **draw** (2.8% of positions won) | no mate exists |
+| KBBK, opposite colours | win, 19 | **win, 14**, all positions won | draw |
+| KBNK | win, 33 | **draw** (0.024%) | draw |
+| KNNK | draw | draw | draw |
+
+   The cylinder result for the bishop pair contradicts a published claim by H.G. Muller. The KBNK result is new as far as we found.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Literature: what is done and what is open
+*Full notes: `notes/lit_cgt_chess.md`, `notes/lit_cylinder.md`, `notes/lit_ml.md`, `notes/codex_referee.md`.*
+
+**Known**
+- **Elkies 1996** (GONC; arXiv math/9905198): numbers including 1/2 and 1/4 on 8×8; \*, ↑, ⇑\*, tiny-1/2, switches; loopy values (on, off, over, dud).
+- **Elkies 2002** (MGONC; arXiv math/0011253): \*2, \*4, ..., \*4096 on large boards via a 3-row pawns game with "stopped files".
+- **Mäser 2002:** promotion modelled as on/off.
+- **Drummond-Cole 2005:** \*2 on 8×8, but it needs pieces.
+- **Siegel:** loopy theory; chess positions of value on and upon.
+- **Gafni 2011:** loopy K+P vs K on tiny boards.
+- **Affine normal play** (Larsson–Nowakowski–Santos 2025): theory for game-ending moves such as checkmate.
+- **Dawson's chess** = octal 0.137 (period 34). The misère version is still unsolved.
+- **Software:**
+  - CGSuite: loopy games are first-class.
+  - Rust crate `cgt`.
+  - Nicholson's `partizan`: alpha-stage chess FEN → canonical forms. **This is the closest existing tool and must be checked before we build B1/C1.**
+
+**Open problems we can attack**
+- \*2 from kings and pawns alone on 8×8.
+- \*4 on 8×8.
+- 1/8 on 8×8.
+- ↑² and ↑³.
+- tiny-½.
+- "Chilled chess".
+- A systematic treatment of en passant, checks and captures.
+- Elkies' "does every \*k occur?" (Nowakowski 2025, B3).
+- Density of \*k.
+
+**Cylinder and torus**
+- Rules exist (Pritchard ECV; Wikipedia), but conventions vary: castling, and the "rook goes round the rank" null move. The null move must be banned before building tablebases, because a free pass destroys zugzwang.
+- Mating-material claims are scattered and unproven.
+- No tablebases, no perft numbers, no strong engine. Fairy-Stockfish declined support (issue #753). There is an unofficial 2026 copy, mtaktikos/CylindricalChess, with loop-based sliders.
+- The only CGT work on cylinders is Domineering (Lachmann–Moore–Rapaport), and it determines only the winner.
+
+**ML**
+- Zhou & Riis (*Machine Learning*, 2026): AlphaZero fails Nim parity. But their "partizan" control was a sum of integers, so **whether nimbers or infinitesimals drive the difficulty is untested.**
+- Husna & Müller (CG 2024): KataGo against CGT ground truth in Go. The number of components was not varied.
+- Missing entirely:
+  - an RL-with-verifiable-rewards (RLVR) environment for CGT;
+  - a train-on-components / test-on-sums study;
+  - an LLM evaluation on zugzwang.
+- Tooling: `mlx-lm-lora` 5.8 has GRPO with custom reward functions, which fits the M4 Pro.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Research program
+
+**Ground rule:** every computational claim is double-implemented, and every proof is checked by two models and by a human (§4).
+
+### Track A: Exact CGT of cylinder pawn endgames
+
+| ID | Question | Method | Done when |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A0 | **Fix the model** | Write the rules down precisely: passed pawn = ±BIG versus Mäser's on/off; race semantics; en passant inside sums; legal versus generalised positions; `sum_safe` positions only in published sums. Long term, adopt *affine normal play* (∞-terminal games) so that "first passer wins" is exact rather than approximate. | A "Conventions" section; solver flags; 100% agreement with the cross-check on single components ✅ |
+| A1 | **Structural lemmas** | (i) **Empty-file invariance:** an empty file stays empty, so components separated by one are exact summands. (ii) **Embedding:** a cylinder position with an empty file equals a flat position on ≤ 7 files, so every new phenomenon lives in **ring components** (all files occupied). (iii) Symmetries: cyclic shifts and reflection, and colour reversal giving −G. | Written proofs, checked by enumeration |
+| A2 | **Resolve the \*2 candidate** | Embed `W a2 c2 / B b7 d7` with real kings (a g-file trebuchet). Verify: (1) the CGT value on the 8-file board; (2) every option's chess outcome, by Stockfish search and, for 8 men, Lichess's Op1 partial 8-man tablebase if it covers the position; (3) a sum test on a wider cylinder or a 10-file board, where \*2 + \* must be a first-player win. Then compare with Elkies' and Drummond-Cole's conventions. | A legal 8×8 king-and-pawn position certified \*2, or a precise reason why the convention fails |
+| A3 | **Value atlas** | Enumerate all positions up to k pawns on n-file cylinders, flat strips and tori (n = 3…8, heights 6–10), modulo symmetry. Tabulate canonical values, outcome classes and temperatures, and diff cylinder against flat. Port the hot loop to Rust (or reuse the `cgt` crate). Publish move-tree certificates. | A public dataset; a table of values realisable only on C_n; minimal witnesses |
+| A4 | **Elkies' open targets** | Search the atlas (and targeted constructions) for \*4, 1/8, ↑², tiny-½ on 8×8 and on 8-file cylinders. Run Elkies' 2002 stopped-file pawns game on **cyclic words**: rotations make distinct options, which may give higher nimbers on smaller boards. | Any one solved open problem ⇒ paper |
+| A5 | **Closed forms** | Values of one- and two-file structures as functions of gaps; the effect of double steps; behaviour as width n grows, with odd n breaking opposition parity. | Proved formulas matching the data |
+| A6 | **Dawson's chess on a ring** (repairs §8) | Solve directly from the pawn rules (`dawson.py`). Prove the reduction from a ring to a path of length n−3. Then attack the **misère** ring version: Dawson's misère problem is unsolved for paths, and the ring may be tractable via misère quotients. | Theorem plus table; misère results as a bonus |
+| A7 | Temperature | Compute thermographs of all atlas components. Measure how often "play the hottest move" fails in sums. Find the maximum temperature as a function of circumference and height, and compare with Elkies' (N−5)/2. | Statistics plus counterexamples |
+| A8 | **Kings: loopy CGT** | Model trebuchets and opposition on cylinders as loopy games (on, off, over, stoppers) using CGSuite. State exactly when "pawns only + a frozen trebuchet = chess" is valid, checking against Track B. Re-examine Gafni's K+P vs K values without edges. | Rigorous validity conditions; 7.2 and 7.3 as worked counterexamples |
+| A9 (stretch) | Formalisation | Formalise A1 and the core canonical-form lemmas in Lean 4 (Mathlib `SetTheory.PGame`). | Proofs compile |
+
+### Track B: Cylinder and torus tablebases, and an engine
+
+| ID | Question | Method | Done when |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| B0 | **Rules decision** | Fix the rules: no rook "pass" around the rank, no castling in endgames, en passant across the seam, no 50-move rule for DTM tables. | Written spec |
+| B1 | Fast move generator | Bitboards: leaper shifts rotate within each rank byte; rank sliders use a [file][occupancy] table; diagonal helices use a kindergarten multiply. Legality = pseudo-legal moves plus an "is the king attacked" test, because there are two routes between squares. Check against Nicholson's `partizan` and the mtaktikos copy. | **Publish cylinder perft numbers**, matching an independent mailbox generator and Jocly/Ludii |
+| B2 | **Tablebases** | Independently re-implement the 4-man results of §1.3, then extend to all 5-man and pawnless 6-man endgames. On the cylinder there are 138 pawnless king-pair classes, and a 6-man table is about 2×10⁹ positions, which fits in 48 GB. Report maximal DTM, mutual-zugzwang counts against Haworth's flat-board numbers, the KPK and KPKP rules without rook pawns, and the torus. | A table of every "can X force mate?" answer, plus public files |
+| B3 | Cylinder engine | Fairy-Stockfish (or the mtaktikos copy) with B1's slider tables and two-route pins and checks; probe our own tablebases in search; train NNUE with variant-nnue tools. | Perft-clean; beats the baseline; plays over UCI |
+| B4 | Bridge CGT ↔ chess | Confirm every king-dependent claim from Track A with B2 or B3. | Every published position engine- or tablebase-certified |
+
+### Track C: ML, RL and LLMs against exact ground truth
+
+| ID | Hypothesis | Method | Metric |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| C1 | **ChessSums: engines and LLMs fail on sums, and the \*-part drives it** | Build legal standard-board positions from k certified components (integers, \*, ↑, switches, 0) plus a locked king zugzwang. Keep more than 7 men so Syzygy cannot shortcut. Run Stockfish 17/18 (node sweeps), Lc0 (Metal backend) and LLMs (GPT-6 via Codex, Claude, Qwen and others via opencode). | Accuracy vs k; the growth exponent of nodes-to-solve; the "parity gap" = accuracy on integer sums minus accuracy on nimber sums |
+| C2 | **Compositional generalisation** | Train on k ≤ 2, test on k = 3–8. Compare a CNN, a circular-padded CNN (exactly equivariant on the cylinder), a transformer, and Deep Sets with a structured latent (a Z₂^m part for nimbers and a real part for numbers). | Accuracy at k = 8 relative to k = 2; does the learned sum obey G + (−G) = 0? |
+| C3 | AlphaZero on Dawson's chess and cylinder pawn games | OpenSpiel or our own PyTorch on MPS; exact values from A3 and A6. Extends Zhou & Riis to partizan sums. | Elo vs "expert" accuracy by value class |
+| C4 | **RLVR for Conway arithmetic** | GRPO on Qwen 0.5–3B with `mlx-lm-lora`, using our engine as the reward. Tasks: nim-sums, canonical forms, comparisons, temperatures, chess position → value. | Exact match on held-out, larger instances; transfer to C1 |
+| C5 (stretch) | Neural or LLM proposers for rare values | A model proposes, the exact engine verifies; hunt for A4's targets faster than enumeration. | New witnesses |
+
+### Track D: Writing and release
+- **Paper 1** (about week 6): "Combinatorial game values of pawn endgames on cylindrical boards." Corrects the 2025 paper and covers A0–A7, plus A2 if it holds.
+- **Paper 2** (about week 8): "Endgame tablebases and perft for cylinder and torus chess" (B0–B2).
+- **Paper 3** (about week 10): "ChessSums: sums of games as an adversarial benchmark for chess engines and LLMs" (C1–C4).
+- **Release:**
+  - `cylcgt` on PyPI.
+  - The atlas and tablebases on Hugging Face.
+  - An interactive explorer: click a position, see its value, thermograph and best move.
