+kings/theory/LOOPY.md:103:| P6, P7 | CHECKED-2 (revised after K19 and K24, 2026-10-27), and partly PROVED in P. **In P:** P6 = (L, W) on cyl:8×8 and (L, L) on flat:8×8 (no zugzwang there); P7 = (D, W) on both boards; in P7 w, Ke5, Kd5 and Kf5 draw and Kf7 loses. The solvers are K19's, the K7 build `kings/delegate/out/K7/scripts/draft/kp_p.c` (its flag is `--pmodel`; `kings/kpsolve.c` has no such flag), K24's `kings/delegate/out/K24/scripts/mine.c`, and the K24 referee's `kings/delegate/out/K24/verify/psolve.py`, the last two written from `kings/SPEC.md`. They agree on every entry and every per-move value (`kings/delegate/out/K24/logs/pmodel_kp_p_8x8.log`, `logs/models_p_bb_bw.log`, `verify/run8.log`). K24 compared `kp_p.c` with `mine.c` on random positions with 0 differences: 11,102 legal positions (cyl:5×6, flat:5×6, cyl:6×6, cyl:7×6) and 769 legal one-pawn positions of the 8×8 boards (`logs/crossval_p.log`; the referee did not repeat these runs). The referee compared `psolve.py` with `kp_p.c` on 1,512 random positions (1,021 legal, seven board and pawn settings) with 0 differences (`verify/xval.py`, `verify/run_xval.log`). **Theorem route for P6, CHECKED-2:** (L1) for the ten sorties with T = 6, 6, 6, 7, 7 (queen clause) and 6, 6, 5, 6, 6 (L1_P) (§5.1); (L2) is vacuous, since the bare group has no context pawn (μ_s = ∞); (L3) holds in the bare group (0 stalemates of s on any line on which s̄ plays only winning moves). The strict form (L3_F) fails in every case, because s may walk its king off F_Z, so the route uses the (L3) of Definition 2.6 only. **In chess:** P6 = (L, W), CHECKED-2 by reduction to KPK: each of the five moves of the side to move is met by the other king capturing the group's pawn (legal in every case; the referee checked this), and the KPK position is won for the capturer in kpsolve exact, in K24's queen-only solver `scripts/kpk2.c` and in `atlas/cylinder/kpk` (the referee re-ran the 12 queries with the atlas binary and with its own kpsolve build). K24 also reports that `kpk2` and atlas agree on all 326,640 cylinder and 331,352 flat KPK positions (the referee checked the table sizes, not the full comparison). P7 b = W in C the same way (Kc7, then Kxe7), on both boards. P7 w = D in C: Lemma 2.7a with EPW and EPB both D for White to move (two implementations), and kpsolve exact agrees; on flat:8×8 the Lichess tablebase agrees too. No hand proof of the P7 w draw exists: Lemma 2.9 refutes (L1) for Ke5, Kd5, Kf5, but (L1) lets White pass, so that failure does not make the sorties safe (open question 7) | `kings/results/king_groups_8x8.txt`, `king_groups_lichess.txt`; K24 (`kings/delegate/out/K24/summary.json`, `logs/`, `VERIFY.md`) |
