+*Part (b)* is the same proof with Q as the starting point: only (I1)–(I5) at Q were used, that s̄'s king is not
+attacked at Q, and that an e.p. right is that of a pawn standing where the right says. These are the hypotheses of
+(b) (they hold for a chess-legal Q, and for the Q of (a) because m and r are legal), together with Lemma 2.2.
+*Part (a)* is part (b) applied to Q: m and r are Z-moves, so
+every pawn is still a Z-pawn on F_Z or a context pawn on a context file and the buffer is empty (Lemma 2.2);
+s̄'s king is on F_Z by the Data; no s̄ context pawn attacks s's king, because m was legal and r moves no context
+pawn; and the only e.p. right Q can carry is one created by r, with its target on F_Z.
+
+**Remark (K3's objection, answered).** K3 wrote that (L2) counts promotions only and that a context passer within
+T ends the game in s's favour. In the D-model a passer is a terminal. Whether it is decisive is (V2), a hypothesis
+of Theorem 1.14 (Proposition 2.11, P11; revised after K10, 2026-10-22), and Theorem 2.7 does not claim it. Theorem 2.7 is a statement about P. In P a
+context passer of s is not terminal. Only a promotion ends the game, and (L2) bounds the first promotion of every
+context pawn of s, passer or not, by T + 1 moves of s. A passer that stands at the end of the window has not
+promoted, and s̄ has. So the objection does not apply to Theorem 2.7: it reads a P-statement as a D-statement.
+
+**What Theorem 2.7 does not give.**
+- *No C-win, and no margin of μ_s gives one.* In P a promotion wins at once. In chess the new queen must survive
+  and the ending must be won. (L1) asks for a queen that s cannot capture at once, which is not enough: that s̄ wins
+  chess after the promotion is the per-position hypothesis (A_Q), checked by kpsolve (§1.2). (L2⁺) is only a margin.
+  The examples below are kpsolve results (`--promote QRBN --cap 2`, both bounds equal; the P values come from the
+  private `--pmodel` copy of Appendix A). By the Labels rule they are data and keep the label CONJECTURE. In each,
+  s̄ is Black, and (L1) with the queen clause, (L2) with the e.p. clause and (L3) hold (revised after K7, 2026-10-22).
+  - μ_s = T + 1, window form with Q = the position: cyl:7×6 `Ka4 ka6 Pf4 pb2`, White to move. T = 1 (b1=Q), and the
+    White pawn f4 has μ_s = 2. Black wins P. **White wins chess**, and 1.f5 is the only winning move.
+  - μ_s = T + 1, form (a): cyl:7×6, Y = `Kc5 ka3 Pe2 pc4`, White to move, m = Kc5–d5, r = c4–c3,
+    Q = `Kd5 ka3 Pe2 pc3`, T = 2 (c2, c1=Q), μ_s = 3. Corollary 2.8 applies (d(Kd5, c1) = 4 = D + 2). After m, Black
+    wins P and **White wins chess**.
+  - μ_s = T + 1, form (a): cyl:7×6, Y = `Kb4 ka1 Pe4 pa2`, White to move, m = Kb4–c5, r = Ka1–b1,
+    Q = `Kc5 kb1 Pe4 pa2`, T = 1, μ_s = 2. After m, Black wins P and chess is **drawn**.
+  - μ_s = T + 2: flat:6×6 `Ke6 kb1 Pf2 pc2`, White to move. T = 1, μ_s = 3 (f4, f5, f6=Q). Black wins P and chess
+    is **drawn**.
+  - In a sampled enumeration of one Black Z-pawn and one White context pawn, a reviewer found draws still present at
+    μ_s ≥ T + 3 on flat:6×6 (4 of 659), and none at μ_s ≥ T + 2 on cyl:7×6 (scratch).
+  c->piece = piece;
+  c->from = from;
+  c->to = to;
+  c->promo = promo;
+}
+
+static void pawn_move(const Pos *p, int from, int to, int capsq, Child *out, int *m) {
+  int us = p->stm, them = !us, last = us == WHITE ? H - 1 : 0;
+  Pos q = *p;
+  q.ep = -1;
+  if (us == WHITE) q.wp &= ~BIT(from);
+  else q.bp &= ~BIT(from);
+  if (capsq >= 0) capture_at(&q, capsq, them);
+  if (to / W == last) {
+    Pos r = q;
+    r.osq[r.no] = to;
+    r.ot[r.no] = TQ + 8 * us; /* the promoted piece's type does not affect king safety */
+    r.no++;
+    if (in_check(&r, us)) return;
+    for (int i = 0; i < nPromo; i++) {
+      Pos s = q;
+      s.osq[s.no] = to;
+      s.ot[s.no] = promoType[i] + 8 * us;
+      s.no++;
+      s.stm = them;
+      if (FROZEN) emit(out, m, &s, 1, us, 'P', from, to, promoType[i]);
+      else if (s.no > CAP) {
+        __atomic_store_n(&cappedSeen, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+        emit(out, m, &s, 1, BOUND == 0 ? WHITE : BLACK, 'P', from, to, promoType[i]);
+      } else emit(out, m, &s, 0, 0, 'P', from, to, promoType[i]);
+    }
+    return;
+  }
+  if (us == WHITE) q.wp |= BIT(to);
+  else q.bp |= BIT(to);
+  if (in_check(&q, us)) return;
+  q.stm = them;
+  emit(out, m, &q, 0, 0, 'P', from, to, 0);
+}
+
+static int gen_moves(const Pos *p, Child *out) {
+        if (r == 0 || r == H - 1) return 0;
+        if (colour == WHITE) p->wp |= BIT(s);
+        else p->bp |= BIT(s);
+        break;
+      case 'Q': case 'R': case 'B': case 'N': {
+        if (p->no >= MAXOFF) return 0;
+        const char *q = strchr(TYPECH, toupper((unsigned char)c));
+        p->osq[p->no] = s;
+        p->ot[p->no] = (int)(q - TYPECH) + 8 * colour;
+        p->no++;
+        break;
+      }
+      default: return 0;
+    }
+  }
+  if (nwk != 1 || nbk != 1 || p->stm < 0 || !epSeen || p->no > CAP) return 0;
+  if (!EPON) p->ep = -1;
+  canonicalize(p);
+  if (!legal_state(p)) return 0;
+  if (p->ep >= 0) { /* must be the square just skipped by an enemy double step */
+    int mover = !p->stm, skipped = p->ep, landed = skipped + (mover == WHITE ? W : -W),
+        origin = skipped - (mover == WHITE ? W : -W);
+    if (skipped / W != (mover == WHITE ? 2 : H - 3)) return 0;
+    if (!(((mover == WHITE ? p->wp : p->bp) >> landed) & 1)) return 0;
+    if ((occupancy(p) >> skipped) & 1 || (occupancy(p) >> origin) & 1) return 0;
+  }
+  return 1;
+}
+
+static char wpov(int v, int stm) {
+  if (v == VDRAW) return 'D';
+  if (v == VILL) return 'X';
+  return (v == VWIN) == (stm == WHITE) ? 'W' : 'L';
+}
+
+static char *strip(char *s) {
+  size_t n = strlen(s);
+  while (n && (s[n - 1] == '\n' || s[n - 1] == '\r')) s[--n] = 0;
+  return s;
+}
+
+typedef struct { char *line; int ok; Pos pos; char res[2]; char *moves[2]; } Item;
+
+static void move_text(const Child *c, char *out) {
+  char a[8], b[8];
+  sqname(c->from, a);
+  sqname(c->to, b);
+  if (c->promo) sprintf(out, "%c%s%s=%c", c->piece, a, b, TYPECH[c->promo]);
+  else sprintf(out, "%c%s%s", c->piece, a, b);
+}
+
+static void run_bound(Item *items, int n, int bound) {
+  BOUND = bound;
+  reset_tables();
+  Child *ch = malloc(sizeof(Child) * MAXMOVES);
+  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+    if (!items[i].ok) { items[i].res[bound] = 'X'; continue; }
+    const Pos *p = &items[i].pos;
+    int v = p->ep >= 0 ? transient_value(p) : pos_value(p);
+    items[i].res[bound] = wpov(v, p->stm);
+    if (WANTMOVES) {
+      int m = gen_moves(p, ch);
+      size_t cap = 64 + (size_t)m * 24, len = 0;
+      char *s = malloc(cap);
+      s[0] = 0;
+      for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
+        char mt[24];
+        move_text(&ch[j], mt);
+        int cv = child_value(&ch[j]);
+        len += (size_t)snprintf(s + len, cap - len, "%s%s:%c", j ? " " : "", mt, wpov(cv, ch[j].pos.stm));
+      }
+      }
+      items[i].moves[bound] = s;
+    }
+  }
+  free(ch);
+}
+
+static int usage(void) {
+  fprintf(stderr, "usage: kpsolve --board flat:WxH|cyl:WxH [--ep on|off] [--promote QRBN] [--cap C]"
+                  " [--frozen] [--moves] [--threads T] < positions > results\n");
+  return 2;
+}
+
+typedef struct { int argc; char **argv; int rc; } MainArgs;
+
+static void *real_main(void *arg) {
+  MainArgs *ma = arg;
+  int argc = ma->argc;
+  char **argv = ma->argv;
+  const char *promo = "QRBN";
+  int boardSet = 0;
+/* kpsolve.c -- exact solver for chess positions with kings, pawns and promoted
+ * officers (Q, R, B, N) on a small flat or cylindrical board.
+ * Rules and file formats: SPEC.md. Written independently of kings/ref/.
+ *
+ * Method. Positions are grouped into layers by (white pawns, black pawns,
+ * officer types). Pawn moves, captures and promotions leave the layer and never
+ * come back, so a layer is solved after every layer it can reach (on demand,
+ * recursively). Inside a layer only kings and officers move, reversibly, so
+ * every placement of them is solved together: counting retrograde analysis on
+ * the explicit in-layer move graph, or repeated passes for very large layers.
+ * Values are win / loss / draw for the side to move; whatever is never resolved
+ * is a draw (the least fixed point, i.e. infinite play is a draw).
+ *
+ * Extra options beyond SPEC.md, for experiments:
+ *   --frozen   kings may not move: a king move loses at once for the mover, and
+ *              a promotion wins at once for the promoter (the pawn-only
