   695	## 2. Frozen king groups
   696	
   697	**Definition 2.1 (king group, buffer).** A *king group* Z consists of:
   698	- a set F_Z of files (an arc on a cylinder);
   699	- both kings and some pawns, the Z-pawns, all on F_Z.
   700	
   701	All other pawns, the *context*, stand on files at distance ≥ 2 from F_Z. The files at distance exactly 1
   702	are pawn-free; they are the *buffer*. A *Z-move* is a move of a king or of a Z-pawn.
   703	- On the 7×6 cylinder with F_Z = {a, b, c}, the buffer is {d, g} and the context lies on {e, f}.
   704	
   705	**Lemma 2.2 (buffer invariance). PROVED.** Before the first promotion:
   706	- no pawn ever stands on a buffer file;
   707	- context pawns stay on context files, and Z-pawns stay on F_Z;
   708	- hence a king standing on F_Z is never attacked by a context pawn, and a context pawn never attacks a
   709	  square of F_Z.
   710	
   711	*Proof.*
   712	- A pawn changes file only by capturing on an adjacent file, landing on the square of an enemy pawn or
   713	  officer. A king cannot be captured.
   714	- For en passant, the capturer lands on the file of the captured pawn.
   715	- There are no officers before the first promotion.
